1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,055
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,055 --> 00:00:08,561
Science, Unit 3: Algorithms. We'll learn
how to plan step by step solutions, and

3
00:00:08,561 --> 00:00:12,899
how to search and sort quickly. After
every lesson, we'll work through a full

4
00:00:12,899 --> 00:00:17,067
example step by step, and we'll finish by
putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,379
A recipe, the directions to a friend's
house, the steps to tie your shoes.

6
00:00:22,829 --> 00:00:24,087
They're all algorithms.

7
00:00:25,237 --> 00:00:29,483
An algorithm is a finite list of clear
steps that solves a problem.

8
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:34,605
Finite means it ends. Clear means there's
no guessing about what each step means.

9
00:00:35,755 --> 00:00:38,443
Computers need algorithms to be extra
clear.

10
00:00:38,893 --> 00:00:43,479
Make a sandwich isn't clear enough. Which
bread? How much peanut butter? Which side?

11
00:00:43,929 --> 00:00:46,873
A computer only does exactly what each
step says.

12
00:00:48,023 --> 00:00:51,074
Every algorithm is built from just three
building blocks.

13
00:00:51,524 --> 00:00:56,633
Sequence: do steps in order. Selection:
choose between steps with a yes or no

14
00:00:56,633 --> 00:01:00,548
question. Repetition: repeat steps while
something is true.

15
00:01:01,698 --> 00:01:04,706
Here's an algorithm for crossing the
street with all three.

16
00:01:05,156 --> 00:01:09,986
Walk to the curb. That's sequence. While
cars are coming, wait. That's repetition.

17
00:01:09,986 --> 00:01:14,287
If the light says walk, cross. Otherwise,
keep waiting. That's selection.

18
00:01:15,437 --> 00:01:18,103
One problem can have many different
algorithms.

19
00:01:18,553 --> 00:01:23,153
Some are faster, some are simpler, and
some use less memory. Part of computer

20
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:25,124
science is choosing the best one.

21
00:01:26,274 --> 00:01:30,858
Your turn. In the step, if it's raining,
take an umbrella, which building block is

22
00:01:30,858 --> 00:01:31,138
used?

23
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:32,783
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:36,753
Selection. The step chooses based on a yes
or no question.

25
00:01:37,203 --> 00:01:40,189
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

26
00:01:41,339 --> 00:01:45,136
Let's write a clear algorithm for a real
job, and run it by hand.

27
00:01:46,286 --> 00:01:51,432
The problem: a cashier owes you sixty
eight cents. Write an algorithm that gives

28
00:01:51,432 --> 00:01:54,969
the fewest coins: quarters, dimes, nickels
and pennies.

29
00:01:56,119 --> 00:01:59,276
Here's the algorithm. It uses all three
building blocks.

30
00:01:59,726 --> 00:02:02,116
For each coin, biggest first, that's
sequence.

31
00:02:02,566 --> 00:02:07,189
While the coin still fits in what's left,
give it and subtract. That's repetition,

32
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:08,880
and the question is selection.

33
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:14,893
Quarters: twenty five fits, forty three
left. It fits again, eighteen left. It

34
00:02:14,893 --> 00:02:17,262
doesn't fit in eighteen. Two quarters.

35
00:02:17,712 --> 00:02:20,080
Dimes: ten fits, eight left. One dime.

36
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:23,069
Nickels: five fits, three left. One
nickel.

37
00:02:23,519 --> 00:02:25,695
Pennies: three pennies, and nothing is
left.

38
00:02:26,845 --> 00:02:31,773
The change is two quarters, one dime, one
nickel and three pennies: seven coins.

39
00:02:32,223 --> 00:02:36,598
The algorithm is finite, because left
shrinks every time, and it's clear:

40
00:02:36,598 --> 00:02:38,516
there's no guessing at any step.

41
00:02:39,666 --> 00:02:44,637
Your turn. Run the same algorithm for
forty one cents. How many of each coin?

42
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:46,282
Pause the video now.

43
00:02:46,732 --> 00:02:50,572
One quarter, one dime, one nickel, and one
penny. Four coins.

44
00:02:51,022 --> 00:02:52,835
Next, flowcharts and pseudocode.

45
00:02:53,985 --> 00:02:57,206
Before writing code, programmers plan
their algorithms.

46
00:02:57,656 --> 00:03:00,622
Two popular tools are flowcharts and
pseudocode.

47
00:03:01,772 --> 00:03:04,374
A flowchart is a diagram of an algorithm.

48
00:03:04,824 --> 00:03:09,393
Ovals mark the start and the end.
Rectangles are actions. Diamonds are

49
00:03:09,393 --> 00:03:13,571
questions with yes and no arrows. Arrows
show what happens next.

50
00:03:14,721 --> 00:03:17,089
Here's a flowchart for checking a test
score.

51
00:03:17,539 --> 00:03:21,904
Start. Get the score. Is it at least
sixty? Yes, print pass. No, print try

52
00:03:21,904 --> 00:03:22,552
again. End.

53
00:03:23,702 --> 00:03:26,689
A loop in a flowchart is an arrow that
points back up.

54
00:03:27,139 --> 00:03:31,609
Here, while the count is at most five,
print the count, add one, and go back to

55
00:03:31,609 --> 00:03:32,344
the question.

56
00:03:33,494 --> 00:03:37,782
Pseudocode is the same plan written in
plain words, set out like code.

57
00:03:38,232 --> 00:03:43,116
It isn't a real programming language, so
it doesn't have to be perfect. It just has

58
00:03:43,116 --> 00:03:43,822
to be clear.

59
00:03:44,972 --> 00:03:46,913
Here's the score check in pseudocode.

60
00:03:47,363 --> 00:03:52,078
Get score. If score is at least sixty,
print pass. Otherwise, print try again.

61
00:03:52,528 --> 00:03:55,344
Indentation shows which steps belong
inside the if.

62
00:03:56,494 --> 00:04:00,419
Your turn. In a flowchart, which shape
asks a yes or no question?

63
00:04:00,869 --> 00:04:02,064
Pause the video now.

64
00:04:02,514 --> 00:04:03,346
The diamond.

65
00:04:03,796 --> 00:04:06,782
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

66
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:12,264
Let's write pseudocode with several
choices, and test it with real scores.

67
00:04:13,414 --> 00:04:18,882
The problem: turn a test score into a
letter. Ninety or more is an A. Eighty or

68
00:04:18,882 --> 00:04:23,035
more is a B. Seventy or more is a C.
Anything lower is an F.

69
00:04:24,185 --> 00:04:26,958
Here's the pseudocode. Check the highest
grade first.

70
00:04:27,408 --> 00:04:29,563
If the score is at least ninety, it's an
A.

71
00:04:30,013 --> 00:04:34,536
Otherwise, if it's at least eighty, a B.
Otherwise, if at least seventy, a C.

72
00:04:34,986 --> 00:04:37,226
Otherwise, F. Only one branch runs.

73
00:04:38,376 --> 00:04:41,618
Test with ninety five. The first question
is yes, so A.

74
00:04:42,068 --> 00:04:45,140
Eighty five: the first is no, the second
is yes. B.

75
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:47,340
Seventy: no, no, yes. C.

76
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:49,838
Sixty nine: no, no, no. F.

77
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:56,333
Every score lands in exactly one grade.
Testing the edges, like seventy and sixty

78
00:04:56,333 --> 00:04:58,774
nine, shows the boundaries are right.

79
00:04:59,224 --> 00:05:04,440
Common mistake: checking seventy first.
Then ninety five would also count as a C,

80
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:06,243
and you'd never reach the A.

81
00:05:07,393 --> 00:05:11,361
Your turn. Using this pseudocode, what
grade does a score of eighty get?

82
00:05:11,811 --> 00:05:13,006
Pause the video now.

83
00:05:13,456 --> 00:05:16,570
B. It isn't at least ninety, but it is at
least eighty.

84
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:18,194
Next, linear search.

85
00:05:19,343 --> 00:05:22,010
You're looking for one name in a list of a
thousand.

86
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:26,662
The simplest plan: start at the top, and
check every name until you find it.

87
00:05:27,812 --> 00:05:30,223
That's linear search. Check each item in
order.

88
00:05:30,673 --> 00:05:35,676
If it matches, stop and report where it
is. If you reach the end with no match,

89
00:05:35,676 --> 00:05:36,753
report not found.

90
00:05:37,903 --> 00:05:42,462
Let's search for twelve in this list.
Seven, no. Three, no. Twelve, yes. Found

91
00:05:42,462 --> 00:05:43,514
at position three.

92
00:05:43,964 --> 00:05:45,116
It took three checks.

93
00:05:46,266 --> 00:05:49,956
Now search for five. We check every item,
and none of them match.

94
00:05:50,406 --> 00:05:54,054
With eight items, that's eight checks
before we can say not found.

95
00:05:55,204 --> 00:05:58,575
In the best case, the target is first, and
one check is enough.

96
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:02,630
In the worst case, it's last or missing,
and we check every item.

97
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:06,046
For a list of n items, the worst case is n
checks.

98
00:06:07,196 --> 00:06:11,697
Linear search has one big advantage. It
works on any list, sorted or not.

99
00:06:12,847 --> 00:06:17,024
Your turn. Using linear search on a list
of two hundred items, what's the most

100
00:06:17,024 --> 00:06:18,202
checks you could need?

101
00:06:18,652 --> 00:06:19,846
Pause the video now.

102
00:06:20,296 --> 00:06:23,411
Two hundred, when the item is last or not
in the list.

103
00:06:23,861 --> 00:06:26,848
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

104
00:06:27,998 --> 00:06:31,241
Let's run linear search on a real list,
and count the checks.

105
00:06:32,391 --> 00:06:37,951
The problem: your contact list, in the
order you added people, is Sam, Ana, Leo,

106
00:06:37,951 --> 00:06:39,132
Mia, Raj and Zoe.

107
00:06:39,582 --> 00:06:42,953
Search for Mia, and then for Kim. How many
checks each time?

108
00:06:44,103 --> 00:06:46,641
Here's linear search. Go through the list
in order.

109
00:06:47,091 --> 00:06:49,907
If the name matches, stop and report the
position.

110
00:06:50,357 --> 00:06:52,491
If you reach the end, report not found.

111
00:06:53,641 --> 00:06:58,441
Searching for Mia. Sam, no. Ana, no. Leo,
no. Mia, yes, at position four.

112
00:06:58,891 --> 00:07:04,005
Searching for Kim: we check all six names,
and none match. Not found, after six

113
00:07:04,005 --> 00:07:04,459
checks.

114
00:07:05,609 --> 00:07:10,477
Mia took four checks. Kim took six, the
worst case, because we must check every

115
00:07:10,477 --> 00:07:12,265
name before saying not found.

116
00:07:12,715 --> 00:07:15,573
Linear search worked even though the list
isn't sorted.

117
00:07:16,723 --> 00:07:19,561
Your turn. How many checks to find Sam?
And Zoe?

118
00:07:20,011 --> 00:07:21,205
Pause the video now.

119
00:07:21,655 --> 00:07:24,941
Sam takes one check, the best case. Zoe
takes six.

120
00:07:25,391 --> 00:07:26,671
Next, binary search.

121
00:07:27,821 --> 00:07:30,381
Think of looking up a word in a paper
dictionary.

122
00:07:30,831 --> 00:07:35,396
You don't start at page one. You open near
the middle, and decide which half to keep.

123
00:07:36,546 --> 00:07:39,661
That's binary search. It only works on a
sorted list.

124
00:07:40,111 --> 00:07:44,628
Check the middle item. If it's the target,
you're done. If the target is smaller,

125
00:07:44,628 --> 00:07:48,196
keep the left half. If it's bigger, keep
the right half. Repeat.

126
00:07:49,346 --> 00:07:52,973
Let's find thirty seven in this sorted
list of fifteen numbers.

127
00:07:53,423 --> 00:07:57,519
The middle is twenty two. Thirty seven is
bigger, so we keep the right half.

128
00:07:57,969 --> 00:08:02,364
The new middle is forty one. Thirty seven
is smaller, so we keep the left part.

129
00:08:02,814 --> 00:08:06,440
The middle of what's left is thirty seven.
Found it in three checks.

130
00:08:07,590 --> 00:08:09,788
Each check throws away half of what's
left.

131
00:08:10,238 --> 00:08:15,797
A thousand items become five hundred, then
two fifty, and so on. After about ten

132
00:08:15,797 --> 00:08:17,534
checks, only one is left.

133
00:08:17,984 --> 00:08:20,650
A million items need only about twenty
checks.

134
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:26,067
Compare that with linear search. A million
items could need a million checks.

135
00:08:26,517 --> 00:08:27,861
That's the power of halving.

136
00:08:29,011 --> 00:08:31,550
The catch is that the list must be sorted
first.

137
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,800
On an unsorted list, binary search can
throw away the half that holds the target.

138
00:08:37,950 --> 00:08:43,132
Your turn. Binary search on a sorted list
of sixty four items. About how many checks

139
00:08:43,132 --> 00:08:44,243
in the worst case?

140
00:08:44,693 --> 00:08:45,888
Pause the video now.

141
00:08:46,338 --> 00:08:51,628
Sixty four, thirty two, sixteen, eight,
four, two, one. About six or seven checks.

142
00:08:52,078 --> 00:08:55,065
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

143
00:08:56,215 --> 00:09:00,503
Let's run binary search slowly, keeping
track of the low and high ends.

144
00:09:01,653 --> 00:09:04,746
The problem: search this sorted list for
seventy three.

145
00:09:05,196 --> 00:09:10,537
The list is: four, nine, fifteen, twenty
two, thirty eight, forty one, fifty, sixty

146
00:09:10,537 --> 00:09:13,047
six, seventy three, eighty, ninety one.

147
00:09:14,197 --> 00:09:16,949
Here's the method. Keep a low and a high
position.

148
00:09:17,399 --> 00:09:21,196
Check the middle: low plus high, divided
by two, rounded down.

149
00:09:21,646 --> 00:09:26,425
If the target is bigger, move low up past
the middle. If smaller, move high down.

150
00:09:27,575 --> 00:09:32,805
Low one, high eleven. The middle is six,
which holds forty one. Seventy three is

151
00:09:32,805 --> 00:09:34,700
bigger, so low becomes seven.

152
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:39,716
Low seven, high eleven. The middle is
nine, which holds seventy three. Found!

153
00:09:40,866 --> 00:09:45,186
Seventy three was found in just two
checks. Linear search would have needed

154
00:09:45,186 --> 00:09:45,474
nine.

155
00:09:45,924 --> 00:09:50,806
Common mistake: forgetting the list must
be sorted. On an unsorted list, binary

156
00:09:50,806 --> 00:09:53,710
search can throw away the half with the
target.

157
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:59,280
Your turn. In the same list, search for
fifteen. What is the first middle item,

158
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:00,791
and which half do you keep?

159
00:10:01,241 --> 00:10:02,436
Pause the video now.

160
00:10:02,886 --> 00:10:07,640
The first middle is forty one. Fifteen is
smaller, so keep the left half: high

161
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:08,432
becomes five.

162
00:10:08,882 --> 00:10:11,144
Next, sorting by hand with selection sort.

163
00:10:12,293 --> 00:10:15,877
Binary search needs a sorted list. So how
do we sort one?

164
00:10:16,327 --> 00:10:20,295
Let's learn one of the simplest sorting
algorithms: selection sort.

165
00:10:21,445 --> 00:10:25,776
Here's the idea. Find the smallest item,
and swap it into the first spot.

166
00:10:26,226 --> 00:10:31,183
Then find the smallest of the rest, and
swap it into the second spot. Keep going

167
00:10:31,183 --> 00:10:32,732
until the list is sorted.

168
00:10:33,882 --> 00:10:35,845
Let's sort five, two, eight, one, six.

169
00:10:36,295 --> 00:10:39,943
The smallest is one. Swap it with five.
Now one is in place.

170
00:10:41,093 --> 00:10:44,848
Of the rest, the smallest is two, and it's
already in the second spot.

171
00:10:45,298 --> 00:10:49,586
Next, the smallest of eight, five, and six
is five. Swap it with eight.

172
00:10:50,036 --> 00:10:55,177
Then the smallest of eight and six is six.
Swap. Done: one, two, five, six, eight.

173
00:10:56,327 --> 00:11:00,410
How much work is that? To find the
smallest, we compare against every

174
00:11:00,410 --> 00:11:01,298
remaining item.

175
00:11:01,748 --> 00:11:07,102
For n items, that's about n times n over
two comparisons. Double the list, and the

176
00:11:07,102 --> 00:11:09,257
work is about four times as much.

177
00:11:10,407 --> 00:11:14,119
Selection sort is easy to understand, but
slow for big lists.

178
00:11:14,569 --> 00:11:19,272
Real programs use faster sorts, like merge
sort, which you'll meet later in the

179
00:11:19,272 --> 00:11:19,689
course.

180
00:11:20,839 --> 00:11:25,083
Your turn. Sort three, one, two with
selection sort. What does the list look

181
00:11:25,083 --> 00:11:26,535
like after the first swap?

182
00:11:26,985 --> 00:11:28,180
Pause the video now.

183
00:11:28,630 --> 00:11:32,256
The smallest is one. Swap it with three,
giving one, three, two.

184
00:11:32,706 --> 00:11:35,693
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

185
00:11:36,843 --> 00:11:40,982
Let's sort a list with selection sort,
writing down every single pass.

186
00:11:42,132 --> 00:11:47,295
The problem: sort nine, four, seven, one,
three from smallest to largest, using

187
00:11:47,295 --> 00:11:48,276
selection sort.

188
00:11:48,726 --> 00:11:51,414
Show the list after each pass, and count
the swaps.

189
00:11:52,564 --> 00:11:57,475
Here's selection sort. For each spot from
the left, find the smallest item in the

190
00:11:57,475 --> 00:11:58,324
unsorted part.

191
00:11:58,774 --> 00:12:02,614
Swap it into that spot. The sorted part
grows by one each pass.

192
00:12:03,764 --> 00:12:07,561
Pass one: the smallest of everything is
one. Swap it with nine.

193
00:12:08,011 --> 00:12:12,811
Pass two: the smallest of four, seven,
nine, three is three. Swap it with four.

194
00:12:13,261 --> 00:12:17,592
Pass three: the smallest of seven, nine,
four is four. Swap it with seven.

195
00:12:18,042 --> 00:12:22,842
Pass four: the smallest of nine and seven
is seven. Swap it with nine. Done.

196
00:12:23,992 --> 00:12:29,542
The list is sorted after four passes. Five
items need only four passes, because the

197
00:12:29,542 --> 00:12:31,949
last item is automatically in place.

198
00:12:32,399 --> 00:12:37,606
Common mistake: swapping with the wrong
spot. Always swap into the first spot of

199
00:12:37,606 --> 00:12:38,778
the unsorted part.

200
00:12:39,928 --> 00:12:44,531
Your turn. Sort six, two, five with
selection sort. What is the list after

201
00:12:44,531 --> 00:12:45,090
pass one?

202
00:12:45,540 --> 00:12:46,735
Pause the video now.

203
00:12:47,185 --> 00:12:51,537
Two is the smallest, so swap it with six.
The list is two, six, five.

204
00:12:51,987 --> 00:12:53,630
Next, comparing algorithms.

205
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:59,559
Two algorithms can solve the same problem.
How do we decide which is better?

206
00:13:00,009 --> 00:13:03,422
We count how many steps each one takes as
the input grows.

207
00:13:04,572 --> 00:13:08,348
We don't time them with a stopwatch,
because computers differ in speed.

208
00:13:08,798 --> 00:13:12,873
Instead, we ask: if the input doubles,
what happens to the number of steps?

209
00:13:14,023 --> 00:13:18,332
Linear search grows in a straight line.
Twice the items, twice the steps.

210
00:13:18,782 --> 00:13:22,643
Binary search barely grows. Twice the
items, just one more step.

211
00:13:23,093 --> 00:13:27,723
Selection sort grows fast. Twice the
items, about four times the steps.

212
00:13:28,873 --> 00:13:32,009
Let's put numbers on it, for a list of a
million items.

213
00:13:32,459 --> 00:13:37,669
Linear search: up to a million checks.
Binary search: about twenty. Selection

214
00:13:37,669 --> 00:13:40,715
sort: about five hundred billion
comparisons.

215
00:13:41,865 --> 00:13:45,854
Some problems are so hard that no one
knows a fast algorithm.

216
00:13:46,304 --> 00:13:51,206
Trying every possible route through just
thirty cities would take longer than the

217
00:13:51,206 --> 00:13:56,229
age of the universe. For those, we accept
a good answer instead of the perfect one.

218
00:13:56,229 --> 00:13:57,803
That's called a heuristic.

219
00:13:58,953 --> 00:14:01,875
So when you compare algorithms, ask three
questions.

220
00:14:02,325 --> 00:14:06,655
Is it correct? How does its step count
grow? And is it simple enough to write and

221
00:14:06,655 --> 00:14:06,976
check?

222
00:14:08,126 --> 00:14:13,219
Your turn. Searching a sorted list of a
million names, which is faster: linear or

223
00:14:13,219 --> 00:14:14,099
binary search?

224
00:14:14,549 --> 00:14:15,744
Pause the video now.

225
00:14:16,194 --> 00:14:19,586
Binary search. About twenty checks instead
of up to a million.

226
00:14:20,036 --> 00:14:23,023
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

227
00:14:24,173 --> 00:14:28,891
Let's compare the step counts of our
algorithms on real sizes, and choose the

228
00:14:28,891 --> 00:14:29,442
best one.

229
00:14:30,592 --> 00:14:35,587
The problem: a school has eight thousand
student IDs. How many steps would linear

230
00:14:35,587 --> 00:14:39,595
search, binary search and selection sort
need, in the worst case?

231
00:14:40,745 --> 00:14:44,009
Here are the rules of thumb. Linear
search: up to n checks.

232
00:14:44,459 --> 00:14:48,470
Binary search: count how many times you
can halve n until you reach one.

233
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:53,357
Selection sort: about n times n, divided
by two, comparisons.

234
00:14:54,507 --> 00:14:56,619
Linear search: up to eight thousand
checks.

235
00:14:57,069 --> 00:15:01,726
Binary search: eight thousand, four
thousand, two thousand, and so on. About

236
00:15:01,726 --> 00:15:02,829
thirteen halvings.

237
00:15:03,279 --> 00:15:08,804
Selection sort: eight thousand times eight
thousand is sixty four million. Divide by

238
00:15:08,804 --> 00:15:11,172
two: thirty two million comparisons.

239
00:15:12,322 --> 00:15:17,208
If you'll search the list many times, sort
it once, then use binary search: thirteen

240
00:15:17,208 --> 00:15:19,768
checks per search instead of eight
thousand.

241
00:15:20,218 --> 00:15:23,674
If you search only once, linear search is
simpler and good enough.

242
00:15:24,824 --> 00:15:29,610
Your turn. About how many checks does
binary search need for sixteen thousand

243
00:15:29,610 --> 00:15:29,858
IDs?

244
00:15:30,308 --> 00:15:31,503
Pause the video now.

245
00:15:31,953 --> 00:15:35,217
About fourteen. Doubling the list adds
just one more check.

246
00:15:35,667 --> 00:15:37,651
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

247
00:15:38,801 --> 00:15:42,748
You've learned algorithms, pseudocode,
searching, sorting and comparing.

248
00:15:43,198 --> 00:15:47,550
Now let's use them all to solve one real
problem from start to finish.

249
00:15:48,700 --> 00:15:51,622
Here's the plan. We need to find a book on
a class shelf.

250
00:15:52,072 --> 00:15:56,360
We'll try linear search, then sort the
shelf, use binary search, and compare.

251
00:15:57,510 --> 00:16:02,490
Step one: the shelf holds five books, in
the order they were returned: fifty eight,

252
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:05,190
twelve, seventy seven, thirty one, forty
six.

253
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:07,027
We want book forty six.

254
00:16:08,177 --> 00:16:10,950
Step two: linear search. Check every book
in order.

255
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:16,607
Fifty eight, twelve, seventy seven, thirty
one: no. Forty six: yes. Five checks, the

256
00:16:16,607 --> 00:16:17,288
worst case.

257
00:16:18,438 --> 00:16:23,174
Step three: sort the shelf with selection
sort. Pass one swaps twelve to the front.

258
00:16:23,624 --> 00:16:28,797
Pass two swaps thirty one. Pass three
swaps forty six. Pass four: fifty eight is

259
00:16:28,797 --> 00:16:29,896
already in place.

260
00:16:31,046 --> 00:16:36,138
Step four: binary search on the sorted
shelf. Low one, high five, the middle is

261
00:16:36,138 --> 00:16:37,105
position three.

262
00:16:37,555 --> 00:16:39,923
It holds forty six. Found in one check!

263
00:16:41,073 --> 00:16:45,873
Step five: compare. For one search on five
books, linear search is simplest.

264
00:16:46,323 --> 00:16:51,507
But a real library is searched again and
again. Sort once, and every later search

265
00:16:51,507 --> 00:16:52,019
is fast.

266
00:16:53,169 --> 00:16:58,057
Your turn. A shelf of one thousand sorted
books. What's the worst case for linear

267
00:16:58,057 --> 00:16:59,868
search, and for binary search?

268
00:17:00,318 --> 00:17:01,512
Pause the video now.

269
00:17:01,962 --> 00:17:06,741
Linear search could need one thousand
checks. Binary search needs about ten.

270
00:17:07,191 --> 00:17:11,266
That's the end of Unit 3. Next, Unit 4:
programming in Python.

271
00:17:12,416 --> 00:17:16,845
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

272
00:17:16,845 --> 00:17:17,237
lesson.

273
00:17:17,687 --> 00:17:18,519
See you there.

